        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, idiomatic mirror of the existing per-concern validation trio already in this package, and slots cleanly into the established pattern.\n\n- **Right unit / right pattern.** The POST pipeline already uses one validate-step-per-concern (`ValidateOwnerFields` for required fields, `ValidateOwnerPostcode` for postcode). Adding `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate` as its own step wired between `validatePostcode` and `normalize` matches that convention exactly \u2014 registration-date validation is not crammed into `ValidateOwnerFields`, which would have made that class a catch-all.\n- **Consistent trio.** `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate` / `FutureRegistrationDateException` / `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler` mirror `ValidateOwnerPostcode` / `InvalidPostcodeException` / `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` one-for-one: single `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto)` method, early-return-when-absent, checked exception in `throws`, escalation YAML named for the FQCN. A maintainer who knows the postcode path reads this with zero friction.\n- **No duplication.** The handler reuses the shared `OwnerFieldErrors` contract rather than inventing a new error body, consistent with the other field-error handlers.\n- **Conventions followed.** Checked exception, escalation YAML under `officefloor/escalation/`, one public method per class, DTO kept at the edge \u2014 all per AGENTS.md. Javadoc depth and the \"only when present / backward-compatible\" framing match the sibling classes.\n\nNothing to change structurally."
